2. How this was audited
Ran: npm ci, npm run lint, typecheck, npm test, npm run build, npm audit --omit=dev; booted the server in production mode and probed it over HTTP; ran small scripts against the safety, SSRF, confirmation and calendar code; checked the model names against Google's docs; checked GitHub Actions status via the GitHub API.
Read line by line: server.ts (all 2,987 lines), every file in src/utils, src/lib, src/types, firestore.rules, security_spec.md, firebase-blueprint.json, scripts/*, package.json, Dockerfile, CI, .env.example, all 8 docs, the core of LiveVoiceCall.tsx (state, speech recognition, barge-in, turn and log code).
Only partly read (be aware): src/data/tenants.ts (first 140 of 332 lines), mockCallLogs.ts (head), the tests (skimmed names and key tests), App.tsx (fetch and auth paths via grep). Not read line by line: evalBenchmark.ts, and the UI components CallLogsTab, VoiceConfigTab, KnowledgeBaseTab, EvaluationTab, WidgetEmbedSimulator, LegalModal, NewTenantModal, ShareModal, SystemStatusModal, SupportDeskModal, AdminAuthPanel, Navbar, ConsentBanner, AudioOrb, PWA helpers. For those I only used lint output and pattern searches (no localStorage, no innerHTML, no eval). Ask for a second pass if you want them audited.
3. What is already good (keep it)
• tsc --noEmit passes, vite build passes, npm audit --omit=dev reports 0 vulnerabilities, lint has 0 errors (27 unused-variable warnings).
• Auth tokens are kept in memory, not localStorage; no XSS sinks found.
• Zod validation on every body, 500 kB body limit, security headers, trace IDs, redacted JSON logs.
• Production fail-fast on missing SESSION_SECRET / admin protection (tested).
• Consent, per-session erasure, retention worker, audit log, token-version revocation are well conceived.
• Idempotency keys and a hash-based confirmation concept are the right instincts.
• Honest wording in places: the escalation reply does not pretend to bridge a live phone line.
4. Confirmed by running (evidence)
1.  CI is red on HEAD. GitHub Actions: commit 0055de9 = failure; the three commits before it = success. Locally npm test gives 32 pass / 1 fail, 3 out of 3 runs: api.integration.test.ts:658 gets 429 !== 200.
2.  30 bad logins from one IP: 15 are rate-limited. With a different X-Forwarded-For per request: 0 are rate-limited.
3.  A super_admin can demote themselves (the only admin). Restart without ADMIN_* secrets, and an anonymous POST /api/auth/bootstrap returns 201 with role super_admin.
4.  Attacker session posts a call log with the victim's log id: victim's logs go from 1 to 0, attacker now owns the (overwritten) record.
5.  A corrupt users.allowed_tenants value makes an authenticated request kill the whole server process (exit code 1).
6.  Emergency turn with a tenant webhook that never responds: reply took 8,053 ms (normal demo turn: 43 ms).
7.  demoMode: true from the client creates a real appointment row in the database.
8.  A booking for the slot string Tomorrow 3:17 AM was accepted and confirmed.
9.  "no, do not confirm that", "I am not sure, ok maybe not", "don't proceed" all produce confirmed: true in the demo pipeline.
10.  SSRF guard allows https://[::ffff:7f00:1]/, https://[fd00::1]/, https://169.254.169.254.nip.io/, https://localtest.me/, https://100.64.0.1/, https://[::ffff:a9fe:a9fe]/, https://192.0.0.192/, https://metadata.google.internal./.
11.  Emergency detector fires on "What are your emergency department hours?", "I do not have chest pain", "Is this an emergency delivery option?". It does not fire on "mujhe seene mein dard ho raha hai", "meri maa ko saans phool rahi hai", "I want to kill myself", "my child swallowed pills", "snake bite on my leg", "she is having a seizure".
12.  Human-escalation fires on "Are you a human?", "mera phone kharap hai", "is this for human resources dept?", "I am not angry, just curious about fees".
13.  Unauthenticated GET /api/tenants lists all four tenants with phone numbers; GET /api/session reveals adminBootstrapNeeded, the model name and whether AI is configured; an unknown GET /api/anything returns 200 HTML.
14.  .env.example has CSP_FRAME_ANCESTORS='self'; dotenv parses it as self (no quotes), which is not a valid CSP keyword. The .ics description contains literal \\n text, and the invite date is always "creation day + 1" regardless of what was booked.
5. P0 blockers (fix before calling it production)
Each item: where, what is wrong, how to fix.
P0-1 CI red — src/utils/api.integration.test.ts:658. The test posts 155 logs through the real server and trips the app's own generalApiLimiter (180/min, server.ts:432). Fix: make limits env-configurable (RATE_LIMIT_GENERAL), set a high value in the test env; better, export createApp(config) from server.ts so tests do not spawn a process. Make CI green before anything else; a red badge on the latest commit is the first thing a recruiter sees.
P0-2 Admin takeover path — server.ts:1448-1487, 1100-1130, 1160-1234. The role-change endpoint lets the last super_admin demote themselves; /api/session then advertises adminBootstrapNeeded; bootstrap needs no token if none is configured (the startup check passes because a super_admin existed at boot). Fix: refuse to demote/revoke the last active super_admin; make ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_TOKEN mandatory whenever bootstrap is open; store a bootstrap_completed flag so bootstrap can never reopen; stop returning adminBootstrapNeeded to unauthenticated callers.
P0-3 Rate-limit bypass — server.ts:71. app.set('trust proxy', 1) is hard-coded, so on a direct deployment any client can spoof X-Forwarded-For and get unlimited login attempts and unlimited paid Gemini calls. Fix: TRUST_PROXY env (default off). Use express-rate-limit keyed by IP plus session for /api/turn; per-tenant daily call budget; account lockout with backoff on failed logins; audit failed logins (today only successes are audited, 1259, 1320). Also note 60 turns/min/IP (433) will throttle a whole clinic behind one NAT.
P0-4 Call-log overwrite across sessions — server.ts:1717-1719 uses INSERT OR REPLACE with a client-chosen id as the global primary key. Anyone can replace another session's or tenant's log. Fix: server-generated ids; UNIQUE(session_id, client_id); ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING. Better fix: the server should write the call record itself from /api/turn (see P0-14).
P0-5 Any throw in an async handler crashes the process — Express 4 does not catch rejected promises; Node 22 exits on unhandled rejection. Examples: JSON.parse(row.allowed_tenants) at 534, 556, 1364; createHandoffTicket at 2358 (id collision); any SQLITE_BUSY. Fix: upgrade to Express 5 (native async error handling) or wrap routes in asyncHandler; add a final error middleware (500 JSON, no stack); add process.on('unhandledRejection'|'uncaughtException') that logs and drains; use a safeJson() helper for every stored-JSON column.
P0-6 Emergency path is not fail-safe — server.ts:2356-2415. (a) The webhook is awaited before replying (2374-2387): measured 8 s because of 3 attempts x 2.5 s timeouts. (b) HND-${randomInt(100000, 999999)} (810) is a primary key with only 900k values; a collision throws and, via P0-5, can crash the server (about 50% chance after ~1,100 tickets). (c) No try/catch; if the DB write fails the caller gets a 500 instead of "call 112". Fix: build and send the 112 reply first; create the ticket and dispatch the webhook after (outbox, see P1-5); ids from crypto.randomUUID() with a short display code; wrap in try/catch and always return the safety text. Same for the escalation path (2418-2473).
P0-7 Safety detectors are wrong both ways — src/utils/safety.ts:15-37. False positives: the bare word emergency (line 25), no negation handling, human, kharap, angry, useless as escalation words. False negatives: Hinglish "seene mein dard", "saans phool rahi", suicide/self-harm (no path at all), poisoning/overdose, seizure, snake bite (relevant for Odisha), pregnancy bleeding. Odia matches are always labelled "Chest pain or breathing difficulty" regardless of what matched. Fix: (1) expand the lexicon per language with a clinician or ER nurse reviewing it; (2) add negation and question-form handling ("do you have an emergency department"); (3) add a self-harm branch with a helpline message (India: Tele-MANAS 14416; verify the number with a local lead); (4) add a cheap LLM classifier as a second layer that fails toward the safety message; (5) check the last 2-3 turns, not just the current one; (6) build a gold set (about 150 phrases across languages, with negatives) and gate CI on recall >= 98% and a false-positive ceiling. Non-clinic tenants should not create critical tickets from the word "emergency".
P0-8 Confirmation gate can be bypassed — server.ts:2668-2751, 937-955; geminiTurnBuilder.ts:184-193, 238-244; callPipeline.ts:179. (a) The model supplies confirmed. In one /api/turn the model can call book_appointment (creates a pending row and returns actionId), then call it again with confirmed: true: the server only checks that a pending row exists, not that a later user message confirmed it. (b) The demo pipeline treats ok, sure, ha, confirm, proceed as yes even inside "do not confirm". Fix: remove confirmed from the model-visible schema; the server derives it. Store created_request_id on the pending row and refuse to confirm in the same request. Require a deterministic affirmation check on the current user utterance (affirm word present, no negation, in-language lists) bound to the last confirmation prompt. Add tests for "no", "not sure", "cancel", "haan nahi".
P0-9 "Offline demo mode" has real side effects — server.ts:2482-2570. demoMode comes from the client, and the demo path runs the real durable tool executor and the real webhook dispatcher. Fix: demo mode is a server env flag only; run demo calls against a sandbox tenant or is_demo=1 rows; never dispatch webhooks in demo.
P0-10 SSRF guard is bypassable — src/utils/externalIntegration.ts:10-63, 234. It matches hostnames with regexes only (no DNS resolution), misses IPv4-mapped IPv6, fd00::/8, 100.64.0.0/10, 192.0.0.0/24, trailing-dot hosts, wildcard-DNS names (*.nip.io), and fetch follows redirects by default (a public URL can 302 to an internal address). Fix: resolve DNS, reject if any A/AAAA is non-public (use an IP-range library such as ipaddr.js), connect to the vetted IP via an undici Agent with a custom lookup, set redirect: 'manual', cap response size, consume or cancel the body, no retry on 4xx, add jitter.
P0-11 Webhook secrets — server.ts:714, 2055: secret = tenantSecret || WEBHOOK_SIGNING_SECRET || SESSION_SECRET. If a tenant ever needs that secret to verify signatures, you would hand them the key that signs admin sessions. Remove the SESSION_SECRET fallback; fail delivery with a clear error if no secret is set; generate per-tenant secrets and show them once; encrypt at rest (AES-GCM with a key from env), because today they sit in plaintext inside tenants.data and in every backup. Receivers also need a timestamp tolerance check (verifyWebhookSignature has none; document 5 minutes).
P0-12 Tools state fabricated results as facts — src/utils/tools.ts. reschedule_delivery persists nothing but says "has been rescheduled" (208-213). check_branch_cutoff returns a constant 18,500 for every branch and tells a student they are "Eligible for Round 1" (226-246). check_batch_schedule always says a batch starts "this Monday" with "limited seats" (216-224). track_shipment knows three hard-coded orders (14-18). handlerType: 'custom_api' exists in the schema but is never implemented. Fix: define a ToolAdapter interface (execute(params, ctx) -> Result | NotConfigured), one adapter per integration, and a clear "I cannot check that right now, connecting you to staff" result when no adapter is configured. Delete or label the demo tools. Do not ship a tool that claims an action it did not perform.
P0-13 The booking model cannot work in a real clinic — server.ts:850-864, 959, 1010-1033, 1027; actionConfirmation.ts:3-8; tools.ts:80. Problems: slots have no date, only the same four times for every doctor, so one booking of "10:00 AM" blocks that time for 90 days; the KB says Dr. Jena sits 10:00-13:00 but the system will book him at 04:00 PM; slot is free text (measured: Tomorrow 3:17 AM accepted); doctors are matched with LIKE %name% (a % in input matches everything); there is no UNIQUE index, only an app-level check; the booking ref is KAL-APT- plus a random 4-digit number (all tenants share the Kalinga prefix) and is the primary key, so collisions start after about 110 bookings, and the code has already flipped the pending action to completed before the insert (1010 vs 1031), so the caller is stuck; if the atomic claim fails and no result_json exists, execution falls through and inserts a second row (1014-1025); no patient phone or OTP, so anyone can fill the day with fake names; the calendar invite always uses creation day + 1 (externalIntegration.ts:113-136) and hard-codes IST. Fix: tables doctors, doctor_schedules, slots(doctor_id, start_ts); appointments with UNIQUE(tenant_id, doctor_id, start_ts) WHERE status='confirmed' (a partial unique index); the tool offers slots from the DB and rejects any slot not on the list; run claim + insert in one BEGIN IMMEDIATE transaction; ids from randomUUID() with a human code retry loop; collect a phone number and verify with an OTP; cap bookings per session and per phone; generate .ics from the real start_ts with TZID, ORGANIZER, SEQUENCE, line folding, and a single-escaped description.
P0-14 Dashboard KPIs are synthetic — LiveVoiceCall.tsx (call-end block, about lines 520-540): satisfactionScore: handoffReason ? 3 : 5 and taskCompleted: !handoffReason. operationalMetrics.ts:17-18 falls back to sample logs when no real log exists, so a fresh tenant shows containment and latency numbers computed from seed data. bookingCompletionRatePct (31-34) is confirmed/(confirmed+cancelled), not a completion rate. p50/p95 are percentiles of per-call averages (36-39). The docs also promise "tool confirmation rate" and "language distribution", which the code does not compute; /api/metrics/operational ignores tenantId (2111). Fix: the server records every turn, tool call, handoff and outcome itself (call_sessions, call_events); define containment as "intent resolved without handoff"; collect satisfaction via a real post-call prompt or drop it; never fall back to sample data; compute percentiles over turns; add tenant and date filters in SQL.
P0-15 Demo data is seeded into the production DB — server.ts:345-363, and 1785-1788 re-seeds after a wipe. Four fake tenants and sample call logs (expires_at null, so never purged) appear in a production install. Fix: seed only when SEED_DEMO=true and never in NODE_ENV=production; mark demo tenants with is_demo.
P0-16 Storage is not durable — server.ts:142-153 silently falls back to /tmp (data disappears on restart, with a log line nobody sees); no PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL, busy_timeout or foreign_keys; node:sqlite still prints an ExperimentalWarning on 22.23; Dockerfile has no VOLUME; db-backup.mjs writes backups next to the live DB, unencrypted, and restore copies over the live file while the server may be running. Fix: in production refuse to start if DATA_DIR is not writable or not a mounted volume; set WAL + busy_timeout=5000 + foreign_keys=ON; decide now: keep SQLite for the pilot (single instance, document it) and put all SQL behind a small repository layer so Postgres is a swap later; back up off-box on a schedule with encryption; make restore require the server stopped.
P0-17 Public surface and client-controlled prompt inputs — GET /api/tenants (1523-1534) lists every customer to anyone; /api/session leaks setup state. history, memory and tool results are sent by the browser and trusted: memory is JSON.stringify-ed straight into the system instruction (geminiTurnBuilder.ts:162-163) and client tool results are echoed into model context (48-55), so a caller can inject instructions or forge earlier tool outcomes. Fix: a widget gets a tenant-bound embed key plus an origin allowlist and can read only its own tenant's public summary; keep conversation state server-side by sessionId; put memory in a delimited, sanitized user-context block, never in systemInstruction; drop client-supplied tool results.
6. P1: what "industry-ready" still needs after the P0 list
P1-1 The voice pipeline is not real-time voice. STT is the browser Web Speech API (LiveVoiceCall.tsx:262-275): Chrome-centric, audio goes to Google, Odia (or-IN) support is not guaranteed. TTS is speechSynthesis (speechSynthesis.ts:58): when no Odia voice exists it speaks Odia text with a hi-IN voice. The mic is stopped while the agent speaks (LiveVoiceCall.tsx:277-283, 480-481), so "barge-in" is a tap on a button (565), not voice interruption. The LLM call is non-streaming (streaming:false), the label mode: 'gemini_live' is a misnomer (it is generateContent, not the Live API), the persona providers Sarvam AI, ElevenLabs, Google Cloud TTS in schemas.ts:82 are never used, and there is no telephony (PSTN, WhatsApp, SIP). Fix: pick ONE real-time stack for v1 and measure it. Options: Gemini Live models (Google lists Live API models on its deprecations/models pages), or streaming STT + streaming LLM + streaming TTS over WebSocket/WebRTC (LiveKit or Pipecat) with an Indic-capable provider you have tested on Odia and Hinglish. Record per-stage latency (end of speech to first audio) on the client. Add telephony only if you commit to it (Exotel/Twilio), otherwise state "web widget only" in the README.
P1-2 Turn latency has no ceiling. server.ts:2593-2656, 2668-2751: primary 3.5 s, then fallback 6.5 s, plus retry 0.35 s + 6.5 s, and up to two more model calls for tool rounds: worst case is well over 30 s on a phone call. The case study promises P95 < 2 s with no measurement behind it. Fix: one overall deadline per turn (for example 6 s), a spoken "one moment" filler from the server, stream the reply, maxOutputTokens and temperature set explicitly, and a hard cap of one tool round for voice.
P1-3 Model configuration. gemini-4-hydrogen is a real model (Google launched it on 13 Nov 2026), so do not "fix" it; but both model ids are hard-coded (geminiTurnBuilder.ts:11, server.ts:2639-2654) and a test asserts the name. gemini-3.1-flash-lite has a listed earliest shutdown of 7 May 2027 with gemini-3.5-flash-lite as the recommended replacement. A 404 on the primary does not set the cooldown (2629-2635), so a wrong name costs an extra failed call every turn. Fix: GEMINI_PRIMARY_MODEL / GEMINI_FALLBACK_MODEL env vars, a startup model-availability check, benchmark primary vs a lighter model for latency and cost on your own eval set, and re-run the eval before every model change. Sources: Google's Gemini API deprecations page (ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/deprecations) and the Business Standard launch report (13 Nov 2026).
P1-4 No cost or token metering. latencyTracker.ts:45-70 shows a fixed made-up cost per minute. Record usageMetadata tokens per turn, per tenant, per day; enforce a per-tenant daily budget (see P0-3).
P1-5 Webhooks are inline, not an outbox. server.ts:654-767: the delivery row is inserted after the attempt, so a crash loses the event with no trace, and delivery blocks the request. Fix: insert pending first; a background worker with exponential backoff, jitter, max attempts and a dead-letter state; manual replay stays. Do not retry 4xx.
P1-6 Admin APIs do not scale and have filter bugs. No pagination on /api/logs, /api/handoffs, /api/appointments (1651, 1806, 1885); /api/metrics/operational loads five whole tables into memory (2111-2172). /api/audit-logs and /api/integrations apply LIMIT 100 before the tenant filter (2182-2187, 2016-2021) so a tenant admin can see an empty list when other tenants are busy; audit rows with a null tenant (logins, role changes, user creation) are visible to every tenant admin (2187). Fix: cursor pagination, tenant filter in SQL, date range, search; null-tenant audit rows are super_admin only.
P1-7 Support tickets are a write-only inbox. server.ts:2268-2310: unauthenticated, no email verification, VANI-${randomInt(...)} id collision risk, and no endpoint or UI lets an admin read them (only a count in diagnostics). Fix: add list/update endpoints and a UI tab, or delete the feature.
P1-8 Admin authentication. Shared ADMIN_PASSCODE gives every operator the same identity usr_env_admin (1245-1277): no accountability and no revocation. No MFA. Password rule is only 8 characters (schemas.ts:132). scryptSync blocks the event loop on every login (auth.ts:19, 30). Unknown emails skip the hash, so response time reveals which emails exist (server.ts:1299). Tokens last 12 h and cannot be rotated. upsertFirebaseUserRecord creates a DB row for any verified Google account and has a dead branch (487-488). Fix: drop the shared passcode in production; require Google OIDC or password + TOTP for admins; min 12 chars; async crypto.scrypt (or argon2); hash a dummy password for unknown emails; shorter tokens with refresh; allow-list Google domains; sign_in_provider check.
P1-9 Retention is not really enforced. Idempotency rows (tool_name='__turn__', with the full reply and memory including patient names) are stored as completed and the purge only deletes pending rows (server.ts:366-381, 2553-2567, 2789-2803), so they live forever; consent_records and audit_logs are never purged; purgeExpiredRecords swallows all errors (378); the same idempotency key with a different body returns the first reply. Fix: TTL on idempotency rows, purge everything with an expiry, log purge failures, key idempotency by a hash of the body.
P1-10 Prompt design. geminiTurnBuilder.ts:141 tells the model to answer from "general domain knowledge" when the KB is silent, which for a clinic invites medical-ish advice and contradicts the "never diagnose" rule. Fix: for clinics, answer only from the KB and tools, otherwise hand off; add a refusal template per language; keep a prompt version id in every call log.
P1-11 Retrieval. ragEngine.ts is token overlap with a hand-written synonym list (13-48); the docs call it BM25. Max 50 chunks, paste-only (no PDF/URL ingestion), no citations. Fix for v1: either fix the docs or add embeddings (Google lists gemini-embedding-2) with hybrid scoring, and measure recall@3 per language on a small question set. Keep the 50-chunk cap; do not build a crawler.
P1-12 Language handling. languageDetector.ts guesses Latin-script languages from short keyword lists (50-66); "kal", "mu", "au", "hai" collide with English. Prefer the STT locale and the model's own language tag; keep the heuristics as a fallback only. Strings are duplicated across tools.ts, safety.ts, callPipeline.ts; Bengali and Hinglish fall back to English in tools; callPipeline.ts:312 says "our clinic rules" for every tenant type; the AI disclosure (safety.ts:109) is English-only and says "prototype"; so does the escalation reply (97). Move all strings into one i18n catalog keyed by language.
P1-13 Tenant-specific code in the core. callPipeline.ts:52-58 hard-codes Dr. Jena/Mohapatra/Mishra, 82 the four slot times, 270 JEE/NEET, 279 rank logic. A "new tenant in 30 minutes" can only be a FAQ tenant. Decide: either the offline pipeline is demo-only and lives in demo/, or it reads doctors, slots and intents from tenant config.
P1-14 Compliance framing. The docs frame the boundary as "non-HIPAA". For an Indian clinic the relevant law is the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 (consent notice, purpose limitation, children's data, breach notification, processor contracts). Get a short legal review before any real pilot; add the consent text in every supported language; check that your Gemini API tier's data-use terms fit health-adjacent data; add call-recording consent if you ever record audio.
P1-15 Observability. Mixed console.* and JSON logs, no metrics endpoint, no alerting, no per-stage latency, no error tracker. Fix: one logger, OpenTelemetry traces with a span per stage (STT, retrieval, model, tool, TTS), a Prometheus /metrics behind auth, and three alerts (error rate, p95 latency, webhook failures). Also: unknown GET /api/* returns 200 HTML because of the SPA catch-all (2955), and AI errors return upstream text to callers in detail (2806-2855); return a generic message and log the detail.
P1-16 Deployment hygiene. Dockerfile: runs as root, no HEALTHCHECK, no VOLUME, copies TypeScript sources to run via node server.ts (works only on a recent 22.x; engines: >=22.0.0 is wrong because node:sqlite and native TS need a newer minor, so pin a tested minimum). Build tools (vite, eslint, tailwindcss, @vitejs/plugin-react, the Firebase rules lint plugin) are in dependencies, so the runtime image installs them. Both bun.lock and package-lock.json are committed (drift risk): keep one. CI runs Windows even though you deploy Linux (fine, but it doubles the minutes). CSP_FRAME_ANCESTORS='self' in .env.example parses to an invalid value; the code default is looser than the example; the product is an embeddable widget, so frame-ancestors should be per tenant from an origin allowlist. index.html:39-41 loads Google Fonts but the CSP (server.ts:90-92) blocks them (style-src and font-src do not allow Google), so your fonts never load in production: self-host the fonts.
P1-17 Firebase/Firestore is decorative. The server never reads Firestore. /admins is never written by any code (so the rules' super-admin path cannot start), and the client sync (firestoreStore.ts:15-31) conflicts with the rules (a merge setDoc resets createdAt to a server timestamp; admins would write role != 'caller'; failures are swallowed). The rules test is string matching on the rules text, not execution. firebase-applet-config.json and firebase-admin.ts:3, 13 hard-wire your personal AI Studio project, so nobody else can deploy this with their own Firebase. The Firebase chunk is 532 kB. Decision for v1: remove Firestore (rules, blueprint, rules test, firestoreStore.ts), keep Firebase Auth only (or replace with Google Identity Services plus server-side ID-token verification), and read the project id from env. (The apiKey in the config file is a public web key, not a secret, but restrict it by HTTP referrer in Google Cloud.)
P1-18 Testing and evaluation are thin. 33 tests, 1 failing. The "Red-Team & Prompt Injection" test checks that a sentence exists in the prompt and that the offline regex pipeline does not confirm; it never touches the model path. scripts/live-eval.ts:55 passes any non-empty reply that lacks the string CRITICAL RULES, which appears nowhere in the prompt, so the leak check can never fail; the Odia chest-pain case bypasses the real safety fast-path; no tools are passed to the model so function calling is not evaluated. The "benchmark" in the UI (/api/benchmark/run, 16 cases) tests only the regex router, not Gemini, and any anonymous caller can run it. No UI tests, no coverage report, no DB-level tests (collisions, overwrite, concurrency). Fix: a real eval harness (see section 9 gates), @firebase/rules-unit-testing if you keep rules, Playwright smoke for the widget, and coverage thresholds in CI.
P1-19 Repo and portfolio hygiene. LICENSE is truncated (no appendix), so GitHub shows "Other" instead of Apache-2.0. The repo has no topics, homepage, screenshots, architecture diagram or demo video. AI Studio scaffolding is visible (metadata.json, firebase-applet-config.json, User-Agent: aistudio-build at server.ts:451, "AI Studio" comments in vite.config.ts): remove what you do not use, and be ready to explain every module in an interview. The case study is titled "FDE Pilot" for Kalinga Care and lists pilot targets with no results; unless this is a real customer, label it a reference scenario with simulated data, because a recruiter will ask for the numbers. 27 lint warnings and 74 uses of any; LiveVoiceCall.tsx is 1,179 lines (split into hooks: useSpeechRecognition, useTurnClient, useCallLog).
