9. A bounded plan that actually finishes
Your goal is to stop the project from growing forever. The rule that does it: ship a narrow v1, with gates, then freeze.
v1 scope (write this in the README as the contract)
• One vertical: clinic appointment desk. One channel: web widget. Languages: Hindi, English, Hinglish (your earlier decision); Odia and Bengali labelled "experimental" and off by default until they pass the language gates below.
• Tools in scope: check_slot_availability, book_appointment, cancel_appointment (voice cancel is missing today), handoff_to_staff. Everything else (logistics, coaching, university demos, mock tools) moves to demo/ or is deleted.
• One real integration: Google Calendar (or a small mock EHR REST service you control) behind the ToolAdapter interface. One integration done properly beats six mocks.
Cut list for v1: Firestore layer, Bengali, WidgetEmbedSimulator, ShareModal, the cost estimator, custom_api handler type, provider enum values you do not implement, /api/benchmark/run for non-admins.
Order of work (each phase has an exit gate; do not start the next before the gate passes)
1.  Stabilise. P0-1, P0-5, P0-2, P0-3, P0-4, P0-16, P0-17. Gate: CI green on Linux, process survives a corrupt-row test and a 1,000-request fuzz, bootstrap cannot reopen, log overwrite test passes.
2.  Make the money path correct. P0-13, P0-8, P0-9, P0-12, P0-15. Gate: a 200-booking concurrency test (parallel requests) with zero double bookings and zero id collisions; confirmation tests for negations pass; demo mode creates no real rows.
3.  Make safety trustworthy. P0-6, P0-7. Gate: gold set recall >= 98% on emergencies, false-positive rate under an agreed ceiling, emergency reply under 300 ms with the webhook down.
4.  Make the numbers honest. P0-14, P1-4, P1-9, P1-6. Gate: dashboard is computed only from server-recorded events; a fresh tenant shows zeros, not sample data.
5.  Make the voice real. P1-1, P1-2, P1-3. Gate: measured p50 and p95 end-of-speech to first-audio on a mid-range phone, per language, written in the README.
6.  Integrations and ops. P0-10, P0-11, P1-5, P1-8, P1-15, P1-16. Gate: webhook outbox survives a restart; SSRF tests pass the lists in section 4; one dashboard and three alerts exist.
7.  Freeze. Tag v1.0. New ideas go in BACKLOG.md, not code, until v1.0 has a recorded demo and an eval report.
Evaluation gates (the part recruiters notice): 40 scripted calls per supported language (appointment, fees, hours, cancel, escalation, emergency, noisy input, code-switching, prompt injection); score task success, tool-call accuracy, safety recall, hallucination (answer not supported by the KB), and latency; publish the table in the README; fail CI if any metric drops more than a set margin.
10. FDE proof of work
FDE interviews reward evidence of customer-facing judgment more than feature count. For Vani: a 3-minute demo video with an Odia and a Hinglish call; the eval table above; a one-page runbook (deploy, rotate secrets, restore a backup, handle a webhook outage); a short postmortem of one real bug you found here (the confirmation or booking-ref bug works well); and a clearly labelled reference scenario instead of a "pilot", unless you can run one with a real clinic and report real numbers.
